  from jsonb_array_elements('[{"n":29,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A school canteen uses 15 kg of rice for 120 meals. A second canteen uses 26 kg for 200 meals.\n\n(a) Calculate the rice used per meal at the first canteen, in grams.\n(b) Calculate the rice used per meal at the second canteen, in grams.\n(c) State which canteen uses less rice per meal and by how many grams, and explain why comparing the totals 15 kg and 26 kg would be misleading.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A school canteen uses 15 kg of rice for 120 meals. A second canteen uses 26 kg for 200 meals."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Calculate the rice used per meal at the first canteen, in grams.\n(b) Calculate the rice used per meal at the second canteen, in grams.\n(c) State which canteen uses less rice per meal and by how many grams, and explain why comparing the totals 15 kg and 26 kg would be misleading."}]}]},"e":"15 000 g ÷ 120 = 125 g and 26 000 g ÷ 200 = 130 g, so the first canteen uses 5 g less per meal. A total is not a rate: the second canteen serves 80 more meals, so its bigger total does not mean it wastes more per meal.","m":4,"df":"hard","tp":"ratio","st":"ratio","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp2-mock","question_number":29,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","strand":"D(i)","next_time_hint":"To compare two groups of different size, compare a per-item rate. Convert units before dividing.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp2-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"Calculate the grams of rice per meal at the first canteen.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["Convert 15 kg to grams first.","Divide the grams by 120 meals."],"strand":"D(i)","answer":125,"tolerance":0,"unit":"g"},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Calculate the grams of rice per meal at the second canteen.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["Convert 26 kg to grams.","Divide by 200 meals."],"strand":"D(i)","answer":130,"tolerance":0,"unit":"g"},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"State which canteen uses less per meal, by how many grams, and why totals mislead.","kind":"text","points":2,"hints":["Compare the two per-meal values, not the two totals.","The canteens serve different numbers of meals."],"strand":"D(ii)","accepted":["the first canteen, by 5 g per meal, because the canteens serve different numbers of meals so the totals are not comparable"],"keywords":[["first"],["canteen"],["meal"],["canteens"],["serve"],["different"]],"minKeywordGroups":4}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark for 125 g. (b) 1 mark for 130 g. (c) 2 marks: 1 for ''first canteen, 5 g less'', 1 for explaining that the meal counts differ.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark for 125 g. (b) 1 mark for 130 g. (c) 2 marks: 1 for ''first canteen, 5 g less'', 1 for explaining that the meal counts differ.","response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"text","response_guidance":"Show the unit conversion in (a) and (b). In (c) give the difference AND the reason."},"marking":"(a) 1 mark for 125 g. (b) 1 mark for 130 g. (c) 2 marks: 1 for ''first canteen, 5 g less'', 1 for explaining that the meal counts differ."},{"n":30,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A photograph is 15 cm wide and 10 cm high. It is enlarged so that the width becomes 39 cm.\n\n(a) Calculate the scale factor of the enlargement.\n(b) Calculate the new height.\n(c) Explain what assumption your answer depends on, and what would happen to the picture if that assumption were false.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A photograph is 15 cm wide and 10 cm high. It is enlarged so that the width becomes 39 cm."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Calculate the scale factor of the enlargement.\n(b) Calculate the new height.\n(c) Explain what assumption your answer depends on, and what would happen to the picture if that assumption were false."}]}]},"e":"39 ÷ 15 = 2.6, so the height becomes 10 × 2.6 = 26 cm. This requires similar (proportional) enlargement. Without that assumption a designer could set any height, and the photograph would be distorted.","m":5,"df":"hard","tp":"proportion","st":"proportion","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp2-mock","question_number":30,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","strand":"D(i)","next_time_hint":"An enlargement uses one scale factor for every dimension. State the similarity assumption when a real object is modelled.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp2-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"Calculate the scale factor.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["Divide the new width by the original width.","39 ÷ 15."],"strand":"D(i)","answer":2.6,"tolerance":0.001},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Calculate the new height, in centimetres.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["Multiply the original height by the scale factor.","Use the same factor as the width."],"strand":"D(ii)","answer":26,"tolerance":0,"unit":"cm"},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"State the assumption and what would go wrong without it.","kind":"text","points":2,"hints":["A photograph that is enlarged without distortion keeps its proportions.","If width and height used different factors, the shape would change."],"strand":"D(iv)","accepted":["the shape is preserved, so both dimensions use the same scale factor; otherwise the picture would be stretched"],"keywords":[["shape"],["preserved"],["dimensions"],["scale"],["factor"],["otherwise"]],"minKeywordGroups":4}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark for 2.6. (b) 2 marks: 1 for multiplying by the factor, 1 for 26 cm. (c) 2 marks: 1 for ''proportions preserved'', 1 for the consequence of distortion.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark for 2.6. (b) 2 marks: 1 for multiplying by the factor, 1 for 26 cm. (c) 2 marks: 1 for ''proportions preserved'', 1 for the consequence of distortion.","response_tools":["rich_text","math","geometry"],"response_requirement":"text","response_guidance":"One scale factor is used for both dimensions in (b) — say why that is legitimate in (c)."},"marking":"(a) 1 mark for 2.6. (b) 2 marks: 1 for multiplying by the factor, 1 for 26 cm. (c) 2 marks: 1 for ''proportions preserved'', 1 for the consequence of distortion."},{"n":31,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A game at a school fair costs $3 per play. It uses a bag of 12 balls: 1 gold ball wins $15, 3 silver balls win $3 each and the remaining 8 win nothing.\n\n(a) Write P(gold) as a fraction and as a decimal.\n(b) Calculate the expected winnings per play.\n(c) Calculate the expected gain or loss per play and decide whether the organiser should expect to make a profit.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A game at a school fair costs "},{"type":"text","text":"$3 per play. It uses a bag of 12 balls: 1 gold ball wins $"},{"type":"text","text":"15, 3 silver balls win "},{"type":"text","text":"$3 each and the remaining 8 win nothing."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Write P(gold) as a fraction and as a decimal.\n(b) Calculate the expected winnings per play.\n(c) Calculate the expected gain or loss per play and decide whether the organiser should expect to make a profit."}]}]},"e":"E(winnings) = (1/12)(15) + (3/12)(3) = 1.25 + 0.75 = $2.00 against a $3 charge, so the player''s expected loss — and the organiser''s expected profit — is $1.00 per play.","m":5,"df":"hard","tp":"probability","st":"probability","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp2-mock","question_number":31,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","strand":"D(i)","next_time_hint":"Expected value = Σ(prize × probability). A loss for the player is a profit for the organiser: state which one you are computing.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp2-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"Write P(gold) as a decimal, to 3 decimal places.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["Probability = favourable outcomes ÷ total outcomes.","One gold ball out of 12 balls, so evaluate 1 ÷ 12."],"strand":"D(i)","answer":0.083,"tolerance":0.001},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Calculate the expected winnings per play, in dollars.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["Multiply each prize by its probability and add the products.","(1/12) × 15 plus (3/12) × 3."],"strand":"D(ii)","answer":2.0,"tolerance":0.02,"unit":"$"},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Calculate the expected profit per play for the organiser, and justify it.","kind":"text","points":2,"hints":["Compare the expected winnings with the $3 price.","The organiser keeps the price and pays out the winnings."],"strand":"D(iv)","accepted":["the player expects to lose $1.00 per play, so the organiser expects to make about $1.00 profit per play"],"keywords":[["player"],["expects"],["lose"],["play"],["organiser"],["profit"]],"minKeywordGroups":4}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark for 0.083. (b) 2 marks: 1 for both products, 1 for $2.00. (c) 2 marks: 1 for the comparison with $3, 1 for identifying it as the organiser''s profit.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark for 0.083. (b) 2 marks: 1 for both products, 1 for $2.00. (c) 2 marks: 1 for the comparison with $3, 1 for identifying it as the organiser''s profit.","response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"text","response_guidance":"Keep the fractions exact in (b) before converting, and state in (c) whose expected value you are describing."},"marking":"(a) 1 mark for 0.083. (b) 2 marks: 1 for both products, 1 for $2.00. (c) 2 marks: 1 for the comparison with $3, 1 for identifying it as the organiser''s profit."},{"n":32,"t":"assess_parts","p":"In a class of 30 students, 17 study French (F), 15 study Spanish (S) and 6 study neither language.\n\n(a) Calculate how many students study at least one language.\n(b) Calculate how many study both languages.\n(c) A student claims that 11 students study French but not Spanish. Evaluate this claim.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"In a class of 30 students, 17 study French (F), 15 study Spanish (S) and 6 study neither language."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Calculate how many students study at least one language.\n(b) Calculate how many study both languages.\n(c) A student claims that 11 students study French but not Spanish. Evaluate this claim."}]}]},"e":"At least one = 24. The overlap is 17 + 15 − 24 = 8, so French only = 9 and Spanish only = 7 (9 + 8 + 7 = 24). The suggested 11 confuses the ''neither'' group with the overlap.","m":6,"df":"hard","tp":"sets and Venn diagrams","st":"sets and Venn diagrams","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp2-mock","question_number":32,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","strand":"D(i)","next_time_hint":"Use n(F ∪ S) = n(F) + n(S) − n(F ∩ S), and remember the ''neither'' group is outside both circles.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp2-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"Calculate how many students study at least one language.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["Everyone in the class either studies a language or studies neither.","So subtract the 6 who study neither from the 30 in the class."],"strand":"D(i)","answer":24,"tolerance":0},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Calculate how many students study both languages.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["n(F ∪ S) = n(F) + n(S) − n(F ∩ S).","17 + 15 − 24."],"strand":"D(ii)","answer":8,"tolerance":0},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Evaluate the claim that 11 students study French but not Spanish.","kind":"text","points":3,"hints":["French only = n(F) − n(F ∩ S).","Compare 17 − 8 with 17 − 6."],"strand":"D(iii)","accepted":["the claim is wrong: french only is 17 − 8 = 9, not 11; subtracting 6 subtracts the wrong group"],"keywords":[["claim"],["wrong"],["french"],["subtracting"],["subtracts"],["group"]],"minKeywordGroups":4}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark for 24. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the union formula, 1 for 8. (c) 3 marks: 1 for French only = 9, 1 for identifying the misused group, 1 for a full evaluation.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark for 24. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the union formula, 1 for 8. (c) 3 marks: 1 for French only = 9, 1 for identifying the misused group, 1 for a full evaluation.","response_tools":["venn","rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"venn","response_guidance":"Fill the overlap first in the workspace, then the two ''only'' regions, then place the 6 outside."},"marking":"(a) 1 mark for 24. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the union formula, 1 for 8. (c) 3 marks: 1 for French only = 9, 1 for identifying the misused group, 1 for a full evaluation."},{"n":33,"t":"assess_parts","p":"Three brands of washing powder are sold in different pack sizes.\n\nPack A: 1.2 kg for $6.60\nPack B: 2 kg for $10.50\nPack C: 3 kg for $16.20\n\n(a) Calculate the cost per kilogram of Pack A.\n(b) Calculate the cost per kilogram of Pack C.\n(c) State which pack is the best buy, and explain why a household that does one wash a week might not choose it.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Three brands of washing powder are sold in different pack sizes."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Pack A: 1.2 kg for "},{"type":"text","text":"$6.60\nPack B: 2 kg for $"},{"type":"text","text":"10.50\nPack C: 3 kg for "},{"type":"text","text":"$16.20"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Calculate the cost per kilogram of Pack A.\n(b) Calculate the cost per kilogram of Pack C.\n(c) State which pack is the best buy, and explain why a household that does one wash a week might not choose it."}]}]},"e":"Unit prices: A $5.50, B $5.25, C $5.40. Pack B is the best buy even though it is neither the largest nor the smallest; the largest pack (C) is dearer per kilogram. Usage matters because powder can spoil before a small household finishes 3 kg.","m":6,"df":"hard","tp":"rate","st":"rate","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp2-mock","question_number":33,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","strand":"D(i)","next_time_hint":"Best buy means the lowest unit price — compute every option before choosing, and check that the quantity will be used.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp2-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"Calculate the cost per kilogram of Pack A.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["Divide the price by the mass in kilograms.","6.60 ÷ 1.2."],"strand":"D(i)","answer":5.5,"tolerance":0.01,"unit":"$/kg"},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Calculate the cost per kilogram of Pack C.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["Divide the price of the pack by its mass in kilograms.","Pack C costs $16.20 for 3 kg, so evaluate 16.20 ÷ 3."],"strand":"D(i)","answer":5.4,"tolerance":0.01,"unit":"$/kg"},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"State the best buy and why a small household might choose differently.","kind":"text","points":4,"hints":["Compare all three unit prices before deciding.","Does ''biggest'' always mean ''best value''?"],"strand":"D(iv)","accepted":["pack b at $5.25 per kg is the best unit price, but a household using 3 kg slowly may waste or spoil powder, so the cheapest per kilogram is not automatically the best purchase"],"keywords":[["pack"],["best"],["unit"],["price"],["household"],["using"]],"minKeywordGroups":4}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 1 mark. (c) 4 marks: 1 for Pack B, 1 for the $5.25 comparison, 1 for noting that C is dearer per kg despite being largest, 1 for the waste argument.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 1 mark. (c) 4 marks: 1 for Pack B, 1 for the $5.25 comparison, 1 for noting that C is dearer per kg despite being largest, 1 for the waste argument.","response_tools":["rich_text","math","table"],"response_requirement":"text","response_guidance":"Give all three unit prices in your working, even though only two are boxed — the comparison in (c) needs them."},"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 1 mark. (c) 4 marks: 1 for Pack B, 1 for the $5.25 comparison, 1 for noting that C is dearer per kg despite being largest, 1 for the waste argument."},{"n":34,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A recipe for 12 flapjacks uses oats, butter and honey in the ratio 6 : 3 : 1 by mass, and produces 12 flapjacks each of mass 80 g.\n\n(a) Calculate the total mass of one batch.\n(b) Calculate the mass of oats in one batch.\n(c) A cook has 900 g of oats and wants to make as many flapjacks as possible, with the other ingredients unlimited. Calculate the number of complete batches possible.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A recipe for 12 flapjacks uses oats, butter and honey in the ratio 6 : 3 : 1 by mass, and produces 12 flapjacks each of mass 80 g."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Calculate the total mass of one batch.\n(b) Calculate the mass of oats in one batch.\n(c) A cook has 900 g of oats and wants to make as many flapjacks as possible, with the other ingredients unlimited. Calculate the number of complete batches possible."}]}]},"e":"A batch is 960 g with 576 g of oats (6/10 of the mass). 900 ÷ 576 ≈ 1.56, so 1 complete batch is possible; 2 batches need 1152 g. Rounding up would promise flapjacks the oats cannot supply.","m":7,"df":"hard","tp":"ratio","st":"ratio","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp2-mock","question_number":34,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","strand":"D(i)","next_time_hint":"In a ratio, divide by the TOTAL number of parts. When scaling a recipe, round down to whole batches.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp2-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"Calculate the total mass of one batch, in grams.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["Multiply the mass of one flapjack by 12.","12 × 80."],"strand":"D(i)","answer":960,"tolerance":0,"unit":"g"},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Calculate the mass of oats in one batch, in grams.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["The ratio parts total 6 + 3 + 1 = 10.","Divide 960 by 10, then multiply by 6."],"strand":"D(ii)","answer":576,"tolerance":0,"unit":"g"},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Calculate the number of complete batches possible with 900 g of oats.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["Divide the available oats by the oats per batch.","900 ÷ 576 = 1.56, so how many complete batches?"],"strand":"D(iii)","answer":1,"tolerance":0,"unit":"batches"},{"id":"d","label":"(d)","prompt":"Explain why the cook cannot make 1.56 batches.","kind":"text","points":2,"hints":["Can you bake a fraction of a batch and still have complete trays?","What would two full batches require?"],"strand":"D(iv)","accepted":["a batch is a whole number of flapjacks: only 1 complete batch fits, because 2 batches would need 1152 g of oats and the cook has 900 g"],"keywords":[["batch"],["whole"],["number"],["flapjacks"],["complete"],["fits"]],"minKeywordGroups":4}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the 10 parts, 1 for 576 g. (c) 2 marks: 1 for the division, 1 for 1. (d) 2 marks: 1 for the whole-batch argument, 1 for citing 1152 g.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the 10 parts, 1 for 576 g. (c) 2 marks: 1 for the division, 1 for 1. (d) 2 marks: 1 for the whole-batch argument, 1 for citing 1152 g.","response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"text","response_guidance":"Do not round 1.56 up — explain in (d) why the answer must be a whole number of batches."},"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the 10 parts, 1 for 576 g. (c) 2 marks: 1 for the division, 1 for 1. (d) 2 marks: 1 for the whole-batch argument, 1 for citing 1152 g."}]'::jsonb) as x
